  "body": "Found during the #1224 trace (PR #1222, row 5), reported by its worker as an open doubt, not a proven defect.\n\n**Observation.** In the app-level trace tests, changing the document's machine, or giving a setup its own machine, did not mark that setup's generated toolpaths out of date: no regeneration was needed between steps. A comment in the UI test `setup_mount_pages.rs` says a mount change clears the cached toolpath; at the app level it did not.\n\n**Why it matters.** A toolpath is generated and judged against a machine (travel limits, spindle range, rotary stack, kind). If the machine under it changes and the cached toolpath and its verdicts stay, the question is what still protects the output: are the machine-dependent laws judged again at post time against the NEW machine (then this is only a stale display), or can a path generated and cleared on machine A post for machine B without being judged on B?\n\n**To do.** Trace it with tests: generate on A within A's limits, switch the setup (and, separately, the document) to a machine B whose limits the path breaks (shorter travel, lower max rpm, no 4th axis), then (1) is the operation shown as out of date / to be regenerated, (2) does verify re-judge it on B, (3) is the post refused by name. Same after undo/redo and after save/reopen.\n\nRated `P3` until proven: if a path that breaks B's limits posts for B without a refusal, re-rate `P1` and say so here.\n\nDone means: the three answers are pinned by tests; a mount change marks dependent toolpaths out of date through the same path other placement/machine edits use (or the issue records why it need not), and no stale path can post unjudged.\n",
